# Modules and packages

A program imports **modules**. Modules are distributed and installed in
**packages**. Some functions in a module are declared in HQL but implemented
in Rust, by the package's **extension**. When you only write programs, you
deal with modules. Packages matter when you install code someone else wrote.

```d2
direction: down

program: "program\nimport ml.metrics" {shape: page}
module: "module ml.metrics\nwhat import names" {style.fill: "#eef6ee"}
package: "package ml\nmodules + extension\ninstalled with hql package install" {style.fill: "#eef6ee"}
extension: "extension\nRust code for the declarations\nwritten without a body" {style.fill: "#f6efe6"}
library: "library\nlibhql_ext_ml.dylib\nloaded when hql starts" {style.fill: "#f6efe6"}

program -> module: "imports"
module -> package: "provided by exactly one"
package -> extension: "carries one"
extension -> library: "compiled to"
```

## Four units

| Term | What it is | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| module | a named unit of declarations, one `mod.hql` or one file under a module root; what `import` names | `regex`, `io.net`, `ml.metrics` |
| package | the unit that is distributed and installed: a module root holding one or more modules, with the extension that implements them | `std`, `hmd`, `ml` |
| extension | the native code of a package: the implementations of the declarations its modules write without a body | `std`, `ml` |
| library | the compiled file an installed package's extension is loaded from | `libhql_ext_ml.dylib` |

A program never names a package. `import regex` names the module, and the
packages installed where the program runs determine which one provides it.
Each module has exactly one provider: a package, or HQL itself when every
declaration in the module has a body.

A package carries one extension, with the same name as the package. A
declaration such as `fn trim(self) : String` has no body in HQL. The
package's extension implements it, and `hql` checks that every such
declaration has an implementation before any program runs.

## Builtin packages

A **builtin** package ships inside the `hql` binary: its modules are bundled
with it and its extension is compiled into it. There are two.

| Package | Modules | How a program gets them |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `std`, the standard package | `core`, `string`, `seq`, `math`, `io`, `regex`, `time` and the rest | the prelude for many, `import` for the others |
| `hmd`, HyperMarkdown | `hmd`, `hmd.vault`, `hmd.search`, `hmd.search.lexical`, `hmd.search.semantic` | `import hmd`, whose prelude imports the rest |

"The standard library" is another name for the `std` package, and
[[book#part-iv-the-standard-library|Part IV]] is its guide. `hql builtins` names the package beside
each module, `collections (std 0.7.0, always available)`
([[13-working-with-hql|chapter 13]]).

## Installable packages

An **installable** package is installed separately. Its extension is built
into a library on your machine, and `hql` loads the library when it starts.
A program then imports the package's modules the same way it imports a
standard one:

```sh
hql package install ml
```

<!-- run: skip -->
```hql
import ml

Dataset([[1.0f], [2.0f], [3.0f]], target = [3.0f, 5.0f, 7.0f]).fit_linear()
```

`hql package install` puts the package under the project's
`.hql/packages/ml/`, or with `--home` under `~/.hql/packages/ml/`: its modules,
the library, and a record of the `hql` version it was built for. A library
loads only into the `hql` version it was built for, so after an upgrade
`hql package rebuild` builds it again.

The source of an installable package is a Rust **crate** named
`hql-ext-<package>`: `ml` is built from the crate `hql-ext-ml`. A package
provides modules under its own name only, `ml` and `ml.metrics`, and never
takes a builtin package's or a builtin module's name.

A package is

- **official** when its crate lives in the
  [hql-extensions](https://github.com/ewiger/hql-extensions) repository,
  as `ml` does;
- **known** when its crate lives elsewhere and the **package registry**
  kept in that repository admits it by name;
- a **path** package when it is installed from a directory.

`hql package install ml` installs an official or a known package by its
name, `hql package install --path <dir>` a path package, and
`hql package list` shows what is installed.

[[book#part-v-extending-hql|Part V]] is the whole of this subject: how a
declaration without a body is implemented
([[32-functions-and-declarations|chapter 32]]), writing a package
([[34-writing-an-extension|chapter 34]]), how its library is loaded
([[35-how-a-library-is-loaded|chapter 35]]), the commands
([[36-installing-packages|chapter 36]]) and the registry
([[37-the-registry-and-publishing|chapter 37]]).
